[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2609076400-west-manifest-regeneration-hazard"
  :adr/title "west.yml drifts from its generator by 67 entries and zero pins — and my first reading of why was wrong"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/body "## What was measured

`nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs --check` exits 1: the committed
`manifest/west.yml` is not what the generator produces today.

Measured 2026-09-07 by copying west.yml aside, regenerating, diffing per entry,
and restoring (working tree verified unchanged afterwards each time):

    67 entries differ
    0  `- name:` or `revision:` lines differ

**No pin would move.** Every difference is group assignment or `userdata`:

    ~65  committed `groups: [gftdcojp]` (or another stale org)
         -> generated `groups: [cloud-itonami]` / `[com-junkawasaki]`
    5    committed `groups: [archived]` + `userdata: {archived: true}`
         -> generated drops both

## Which side is right: the generator

The group changes are corrections. `action-paid-outreach`, `app-6ir`, `app-ka`
and the rest **live at `orgs/cloud-itonami/…`** while west.yml still calls them
`gftdcojp`. The generator derives the group from the path, so it is reading the
world as it is; the committed file is carrying names from before the gftd
retirement moved those repositories. Same for the five `archived` entries:
`ai-kaisya`, `kami-engine-render`, `kami-engine-io` and the others are marked
archived in west.yml but **their current paths are not among the twenty
`:archived` keys in `repos.edn`**, so the policy no longer declares them
archived and the generator stops emitting it.

## I had this backwards first, and the way I found out is the point

The first pass concluded the opposite — that regenerating would strip DataLad
`userdata` from `m365-archive`, `product-corpus`, `llm-dataset` and
`emr-claims-primary-sources`, unisolating four large-binary datasets from
default `west update`. That would have been severe, so it was checked rather
than reported.

It was false. `m365-archive`'s committed and generated blocks are **byte
identical**, `groups: [datalad]` and `userdata: {datalad: true}` included, and
its `:datalad` policy key matches its west path exactly.

The error was in how the affected entries were identified: `grep -B12
'datalad: true'` on the committed file, which names entries that merely sit
NEAR a marker rather than entries that appear in the diff. Proximity in a file
is not membership in a change set. Comparing per-entry blocks gave 67 names,
and none of the four was among them.

**壊したものと報告されたものが一致することを確かめる** applies to reading a
diff, not only to breaking a check on purpose.

## What is still open

Whether those five entries *should* still be archived. The generator is
faithfully following `repos.edn`, but whether the policy is right — genuinely
un-archived, or lost the key when a repo moved path — is intent, not
measurement, and this ADR does not settle it. The consequence of regenerating
is that five repositories leave the `archived` group and re-enter the default
`west update` scope.

## The standing instruction still holds, for a different reason

CLAUDE.md's layout quickstart shows the bare `nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs`;
its Git-operations section forbids wholesale regeneration commits, after the
incident where one registration flushed 44 broken pins to main. That prohibition
is about **pins**, and this drift touches none — but a 67-entry bulk change is
still unreviewable as one commit, and it silently answers the archived question
above by fiat.

So: keep using `--entry <name>`. Three repositories were registered that way
today (`cloud-itonami-cli`, `cloud-itonami-apex`, plus pin advances), each a
five-line diff with server-side pin verification. None is implicated here —
their entries match their checkouts, and a sample of 401 checked-out projects
found zero differing from their pins, so this is not checkout staleness.

## Next step

Decide the archived question for those five, then land the group corrections
deliberately — ideally `--entry` per repository, or one commit that says in its
message that it is the gftd-retirement group catch-up and lists the five."}]
